direction: down

title: |md
  ## sched_ext: BPF as the kernel scheduler
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

cfs: "Default scheduler (CFS)" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  c_body: |md
    Single in-kernel implementation:
    - kernel/sched/fair.c
    - red-black tree of runnable tasks
    - virtual runtime accounting
    - load balancing across CPUs
    - hardcoded policy

    To change policy: kernel patch.
  |
}

scx: "sched_ext (BPF scheduler)" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  s_body: |md
    Pluggable scheduler via struct_ops:
    - kernel/sched/ext.c (framework)
    - your BPF program implements callbacks:
      enqueue, dispatch, init, exit
    - load BPF object → scheduler activates
    - watchdog catches stalls → falls back to CFS

    Iterate scheduling policy at userspace speed.
  |
}

watchdog: |md
  **The watchdog is mandatory.** A faulty BPF scheduler that
  fails to dispatch tasks would freeze the system. The kernel
  monitors tasks waiting > 30 seconds without dispatch and
  ejects the BPF scheduler, falling back to CFS.

  This safety net is what makes BPF scheduling shippable.
| {style.fill: "#fef3c7"}

cfs -> scx
scx -> watchdog
